    {
      "id": "b-figure-1",
      "type": "FIGURE",
      "page": 2,
      "section": "Results",
      "components": [
        {
          "original_block": 97,
          "bbox": [
            39.68220520019531,
            284.0237121582031,
            294.2340087890625,
            352.7767028808594
          ],
          "text": "Fig. 1 | Electronic properties of Sb 0112\n(\n)–MoS2 and Sb (0001)–MoS2 contacts \nfrom DFT calculations. a,b, Atomic-projected electronic band structure of Sb \n(0112)–MoS2 (a) and Sb (0001)–MoS2 (b) contacts. The red and blue symbols \nrepresent orbitals from MoS2 and Sb, respectively. The size of the symbols \nrepresents the relative contribution. c,d, The charge density near EF (left) and \nthe differential charge density (right) of Sb (0112) (c) and Sb (0001) (d) contacts \n(red, positive; blue, negative). The blue, green and yellow spheres represent  "
        },
        {
          "original_block": 96,
          "bbox": [
            306.13946533203125,
            283.0007019042969,
            562.3009643554688,
            351.6423034667969
          ],
          "text": "Sb, Mo and S atoms, respectively. The differential charge density iscalculated \nby subtracting pre-contact three-layer Sb and MoS2 charge density from the \npost-contact charge density. The isosurface level is 9 × 10−5 e Bohr−3 and \n7 × 10−4 e Bohr−3 for the left and right panels, respectively. e, Projected PLDOS of \ndifferent orbitals of Sb (0112)–MoS2 and Sb (0001)–MoS2 contacts near EF. f, The \nintegrated DOS of TMDs at EF. g, The charge transfer from Sb to TMDs by Bader \ncharge analysis. h, The interfacial vdW interaction between TMDs and Sb."
        }
      ],
      "number": "1",
      "quantities": [],
      "epistemic_status": "PAPER_EXPLICIT",
      "review_origin": "MODEL_SOURCE_REVIEW",
      "human_review": "PENDING",
      "channel": "CAPTION_EXPLICIT",
      "visual_inferences": []
    },
    {
      "id": "b-figure-2",
      "type": "FIGURE",
      "page": 2,
      "section": "Results",
      "components": [
        {
          "original_block": 132,
          "bbox": [
            39.681610107421875,
            695.92236328125,
            291.4875793457031,
            745.3051147460938
          ],
          "text": "Fig. 2 | Characterization of the Sb 0112\n(\n)–MoS2 contact. a, Raman spectrum \nof Sb film deposited on MoS2/Si substrate shows Sb Eg and A1g peaks at 111 cm−1 \nand 149 cm−1, respectively. b, XRD θ–2θ diffractogram of Sb film with different \ndeposition conditions and substrate. RT, room temperature. c, Cross-section \nHAADF-STEM image of the Sb (0112)–MoS2 contact. The interplane distance of "
        },
        {
          "original_block": 131,
          "bbox": [
            306.1414794921875,
            696.0575561523438,
            558.864013671875,
            744.8113403320312
          ],
          "text": "0.315 nm for Sb (0112) planes is marked. Scale bar, 2 nm. d, Zoom-in \natomic-resolution image from the red box in c. The Mo, S and Sb atoms are \noverlayed on the image. The vdW gap of 0.285 nm is marked. Scale bar, 1 nm.  \ne, Optical microscope image of Sb films deposited on MoS2 and SiO2 substrate, \nshowing clear colour contrast. Scale bar, 20 μm."
        }
      ],
      "number": "2",
      "quantities": [],
      "epistemic_status": "PAPER_EXPLICIT",
      "review_origin": "MODEL_SOURCE_REVIEW",
      "human_review": "PENDING",
      "channel": "CAPTION_EXPLICIT",
      "visual_inferences": []
    },
    {
      "id": "b-figure-3",
      "type": "FIGURE",
      "page": 3,
      "section": "Results",
      "components": [
        {
          "original_block": 65,
          "bbox": [
            39.682498931884766,
            262.4661560058594,
            294.635986328125,
            331.8489074707031
          ],
          "text": "Fig. 3 | Electrical properties and stability of the Sb 0112\n(\n)–MoS2 contact. \n a, Transfer curves of a typical TLM structure with Lc ranging from 0.1 μm to \n1.5 μm and Vds = 0.1 V. Inset: false-colour scanning electron microscope image \nof a TLM structure. Scale bar, 2 μm. b, Rc extraction using the TLM method from \nthe device in a. From top to bottom, n2D = 1.2 × 1013 cm−2, 1.6 × 1013 cm−2, \n2.1 × 1013 cm−2 and 3 × 1013 cm−2, respectively. Inset: the magnified data plot near \nLc = 0, where the y intercept and x intercept represent 2Rc and 2LT, respectively. "
        },
        {
          "original_block": 64,
          "bbox": [
            306.1393127441406,
            261.412109375,
            560.2733764648438,
            330.07781982421875
          ],
          "text": "It is noted that the LT derived by the TLM method assumes the same MoS2 sheet \nresistivity underneath and outside the contact, which is probably violated \nconsidering the charge transfer between Sb (0112) and MoS2. This could lead to \nuncertainties in the obtained LT. c. Rc (red squares) and intrinsic mobility (black \nrhombuses) as a function of temperature for another device. d–f, Rc (d), LT (e) \nand μint (f) distribution of the Sb (0112) and Sb (0001) contacts, respectively. \nThe solid lines are Gaussian fittings."
        }
      ],
